I've been a gamer on and off all my life. There are few franchises (
) I am still a faithful follower of - and it is due to their hard work and constant reaching out to the community that follow them. I've observed the Fallout series since Fallout 3 and much like it's Fantasy counterpart (
) have fallen in love with the story. Unlike some games of recent where the gameplay is ok at best and the mechanics are even outstanding with out of this world graphics, however. If the game hasn't a good story to tell with all the other factors of what makes a good game now a days, all the hard work in modeling, texturing and making the world of said game becomes a mute point. If your game isn't good for anything other than taking a couple screenshots that are really good, you let down the artists who worked on it and the fans/ the consumer.
With that said, I would too state that Fallout 4 (
) is what I believe a benchmark of what a great game should be (
). And wile I had initially played this on a friend's PS4, I have purchased the game for myself on PC. However this is not about Console V.s PC or anything like that. This is purely based on the game itself.

(I'm the sort of person who looks really closely at graphics and while I had seen a few seeming issues they were not so big that it bothered me enough to complain about them. I was really impressed with some of the lengths that some textures went to. As a 3d artist myself, I know that sometimes seeming issues can occur unintentionally and don't get noticed until way down the creative process and that sometimes doing a simple retexture could result in a massively time consuming and complex workaround for something as simple as a few pixels.
When Fallout 4 was first released a good friend of mine had taken a screenshot of it and posted it to Social Media. I had commented something the like of "Wow this photo makes me think of Fallout" I had legitimately thought it was a photo that was taken of some place and had a filter slapped on it. He had later let me know that it was indeed a screenshot of Fallout 4 by replying "You're not wrong Bradda!")

(Music helps convey the story even better to us by it's ques. Reassuring us that the moment is sad, foreboding, triumphant or that it's time for action. The music in Fallout 4 made me feel like a BOSS when a goal were accomplished.
The voice acting was rather great as well but it often did indeed feel scripted or forced. One can't always have the luxury of having all the VA's (
) in the same room for scenes or 'encounters.' The overall quality of the dialogue is usually only as good as the initial dialogue the other Va's have to work from.
Overall, I enjoyed the music and also the VA. If I had to choose one particular character purely based on their conveyance of the character, it would be Nick Valentine. Even though I am 32 years old, I had grown up listening to 50's 60's and 70's music and had read D.i.c.k. Tracey comics. Nick was a callback to how I felt detectives of the early half of the 1900's narrated their internal monologue.)

(I didn't initially notice any control differences because I had played Fallout 3 and  Fallout New Vegas on PC. However Bethesda has always been really good at making their control setups manageable through key binds. (
)
The one thing that did irk me a bit was sometimes during the interactive dialogue between yourself and another character; the camera wouldn't always behave. Several times I had been inside one of the character's heads and the odd sight of floating eyeballs and teeth were quite a laugh however took away from my experience of the story.
Other times it was random FOV (
) shifts. I should add that these issues could have been a factor of how my computer was interpreting the .INI files and algorithms.
Another bit of the gameplay/mechanics I'd like to tip my hat to, is the building mode. Though it was often tricky to get things just right in alignment or rotation, and often things wouldn't 'snap' into place as you expected. The overall feel and ability to build your simple shack or corrugated castle surrounded with turrets was something that made the cake all the sweeter.)

(While this review is something I should have done a long time ago due to time of relevance, the story of Fallout 4 while not entirely original with the concept of looking for ones child in a world that the protagonist doesn't fully recognize - it was conveyed in such an original way.


The end game wasn't a complete shock. I'll give no spoilers but the rout in which I took in the game was ultimately satisfying. As I had played the way I would, had I personally been in the protagonists situation (
)
I don't quite trust -in heart of hearts- the Brotherhood of Steel. Nor do I in -heart of hearts- trust The institute. And while I believe under the right circumstances that Advanced AI could achieve some sense of awareness and self awareness - sentience, I had decided to stick with the faction I originally had encountered for my original gameplay which were the Minutemen.
I thoroughly enjoyed each character including the ones that annoyed me. I enjoyed the ability to pursue relationships with your followers (
) My personally favorite follower (
) was Curie and while I didn't follow her line till it's "conclusion" it is definitely going to be a priority on my next play through as a mechanical engineer. I look forward to getting and playing through with the DLC's (
)
[EDIT] I wanted to append this review only to note that I reviewed this purely on it's own merrits - meaning the product at it's factory vanilla content. The creation club thing is it's own review entirely.

